<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="materials">
  <title>Textbook and Lecture Notes</title>

  <p>
    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Title</title>
        <p><pubtitle>Thomas' Calculus: Early Transcendentals</pubtitle></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Authors</title>
        <p>Joel Hass, Christopher Heil, Przemyslaw Bogacki, and Maurice Weir</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Edition</title>
        <p>15th edition</p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>The textbook is optional.</alert> You are not required to purchase
    it. In this course we will be using my lecture notes, which are posted on
    the
    <!-- TODO: the "shorttitlelowercase.html" in this address looks like an  -->
    <!-- unfilled PreTeXt placeholder from the lecture-notes project.       -->
    <!-- Confirm the real landing page before the quarter starts.           -->
    <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Mehdi/shorttitlelowercase.html">MATH 13 course website</url>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The lecture notes cover all of the material we discuss in class, and they
    are the primary reference for this course. The textbook is a good
    supplementary resource if you would like additional worked examples and
    practice problems, but everything you need for the assignments and exams
    will be covered in the lecture notes and in class.
  </p>

  <p>
    The notes are posted as <term>skeletal notes</term> on the
    <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Skeletal-Notes/shorttitlelowercase.html">MATH 13 skeletal notes site</url>
    <mdash/> see, for example, the
    <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Skeletal-Notes/worksheet-assignment-1.html">Assignment 1 page</url>. Each section contains
    the definitions, statements of theorems, and the outline of every example
    and proof, but the details are left blank for us to fill in together during
    class. Print or download the skeletal notes for an upcoming topic and bring
    them with you, and complete them in class as we work through the material.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything for this course is posted in the skeletal notes: the notes
    themselves, the assignments, the review problems for each exam, and the
    solutions. Solutions to an assignment are posted only after its due date
    has passed. Each item is added to the skeletal notes as we reach it, so
    check back regularly; every posting is also logged, with a direct link and
    the date it went up, in <xref ref="posted-materials"/>.
  </p>
</section>
